course: myths
lesson: 24
title: School legends
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Oscar swears a kid at his cousin's school skipped a whole year and nobody noticed. Every school has legends like this: the haunted toilets, the secret tunnel, the head who sleeps in the office.
explain: A school legend is usually told about "a kid at my cousin's school", never about someone you can actually ask; that friend-of-a-friend detail is the first clue it may be made up.
explain: Real school life runs on rules and records: the register is taken every day, absences are followed up, and big decisions, like closing for snow, are made by the head, not by a magic law.
explain: Some legends hold a grain of truth: schools do keep records, and chewing gum may wake you up a little for a short time; but the scary or magic part is almost always added.
explain: To check one, ask a teacher, the school office or the person it happened to, and enjoy the spooky ones as stories, without teasing the younger kids who believe them.
fact: Schools take a register every day, and an absence is usually followed up quickly by phone calls, letters or visits, so a pupil missing a whole year would be noticed.
fact: Schools do keep records about pupils, but there is no single "permanent record" that every future employer (a boss who gives you a job) reads, and small mistakes are not carried for life.
fact: There is no rule that a class can leave if the teacher is 15 minutes late; you should stay and send someone to tell the office.
fact: There is no law that closes schools after a certain amount of snow; the head teacher decides, based on safety.
fact: There is no evidence that ghosts exist; haunted-toilet stories are told at schools in many countries, such as Japan's "Hanako-san".
fact: Pencil "lead" is graphite (a soft, grey, shiny form of carbon) mixed with clay and contains no lead, so it cannot give you lead poisoning; a pencil stab should still be washed and checked because it could get infected.
fact: Teachers are pleased by a score of 100%; a perfect mark is not a sign of cheating.
fact: There is no evidence that Friday the 13th brings bad luck, though worrying about it could distract you.
fact: Head teachers go home at night like other people; they do not live in their offices.
fact: Some small studies found chewing gum can make you feel a little more alert (awake and paying attention) for a short time, but the results are mixed, and gum gives no magic marks; many schools do not allow it.
fact: Secret tunnels between old buildings are a very common legend, and they almost never turn out to be real; old maps and local history groups can help you check.
fact: A story about "a friend of a friend" that nobody can check is called an urban legend.
exercise: Could a pupil skip a whole year of school without anyone noticing? | No, the register is taken daily and absences are followed up | Yes, it happens in most big schools every year | Yes, if they sit at the back of the class | Only if the pupil's name is spelt wrong
exercise: Does everything you do go on a permanent record that follows you for life? | No, small mistakes are not carried for life | Yes, every employer reads every school record | Yes, but only things written in red pen | No, because schools keep no records at all
exercise: What should a class do if the teacher is 15 minutes late? | Stay and send someone to tell the office | Leave, because the rule says so | Go home straight away and tell your parents | Go to the playground until the bell
exercise: Who decides whether a school closes for snow? | The head teacher, based on safety | A law that sets 10 centimetres | The pupils, by voting in the morning | The weather forecast on television
exercise: What is pencil "lead" made of? | Graphite mixed with clay | Real lead metal from a mine | Coal mixed with fine sand | Black paint dried very hard
exercise: Why can't a pencil stab give you lead poisoning? | Pencils contain no lead | The lead is too small to matter | The lead stays inside the wood | Your skin filters out the lead
exercise: What should you still do after a pencil stab? | Wash it and get it checked, as it could get infected | Nothing, pencil wounds can never get infected | Leave the tip in so it can heal | Cover it with ink to stop germs
exercise: What does a teacher usually think about a 100% score? | They are pleased | They think you cheated | They make you take the test again | They lower your mark to be fair
exercise: Is there evidence that Friday the 13th brings bad luck? | No, but worrying about it could distract you | Yes, many more tests are failed on that day | Yes, schools close on Friday the 13th | Only if it falls in the month of October
exercise: Where do head teachers sleep? | At home, like other people | In their office at school | In the school hall on camp beds | In a secret flat above the library
exercise: What do studies say about chewing gum in tests? | It may make you a little more alert briefly; results are mixed | It always raises your marks by ten percent | It makes you forget everything you learned | It is proven to make you finish faster
exercise: How could you check the legend of a secret tunnel from school? | Look at old maps or ask a local history group | Dig under the playground at night | Ask the youngest pupils in the school what they heard | Believe it because the building is old
exercise: What is an urban legend? | A story about a "friend of a friend" that nobody can check | A true story printed in a newspaper | A rule written by the government | A book of facts about big cities
exercise: How should you treat younger kids who believe the haunted-toilet story? | Enjoy it as a story but don't tease or scare them | Scare them even more so they stay out of the toilets | Laugh at them in front of the whole class | Tell them it is true so they behave
test: "a kid at my cousin's school bunked off for a YEAR and no one noticed" What's the first clue it's a legend? | It's about a friend of a friend nobody can ask | It mentions a cousin, and everybody knows cousins always lie | It's about a school, and schools are boring | It happened a long time ago
test: Your big brother says one detention will stop you getting any job ever. What's true? | There's no lifelong record that every employer reads | He's right, every single detention gets sent to the police | Detentions are published in the newspaper | Only detentions on Mondays count
test: "teacher's 15 mins late, rule says we can go, come on" What should the class do? | Stay and send someone to the office | Go, because it's the law | Hide in the toilets until home time | Leave a note and walk home
test: "it's snowed 10cm, school HAS to close by law!" Is that how it works? | No, the head decides based on safety | Yes, it's a law in every single country | Only if the snow is fresh, deep and white | Yes, but only for the primary schools
test: Jack stabbed himself with a pencil and is panicking about lead poisoning. What do you tell him? | Pencils have no lead; wash it and tell an adult | He needs an emergency injection now | Pull out the lead with a magnet | Pencils only poison you if they are painted yellow
test: Mason got 100% and is scared the teacher will think he cheated. What is most likely? | The teacher will be pleased | The teacher will call his parents | The teacher will tear up the test | The teacher will give him 0%
test: "i'm gonna fail, it's friday the 13th!!" What could you tell your mate? | No evidence for that; worrying is the bigger problem | You're right, so there's no point bothering to revise this week | Wear a lucky sock and you'll be fine | Only people born on the 13th get unlucky
test: Harvey swears the head teacher sleeps in her office. How could you check politely? | Ask her, or notice she arrives each morning like everyone else | Hide in school overnight to see | Look through her office window at night | Believe it because Harvey is your best mate
test: "chew gum in the exam = top marks, science says" What does the science really say? | A small, brief alertness boost at most, and mixed results | Top marks every time, proven many times | Gum makes your brain bigger for an hour | Gum has nothing to do with being alert ever
test: Nadia's little sister is too scared to use the school toilets because of the ghost story. What's kind? | Tell her it's just a story and go with her | Tell her the ghost only comes out at lunch | Laugh and tell everyone she's scared | Tell her to hold it until home time
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of the core of a pencil. | It consists of graphite combined with clay | It consists of the metal lead, as the name says | It consists of finely compressed coal and soot | It consists of black paint dried into a rod
exam: Identify the party by whom the closure of a school on account of snowfall is ordinarily determined. | The head teacher, having regard to safety | A statute prescribing a fixed depth of snow | The pupils, by means of a ballot | The national weather service
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term urban legend? | An account attributed to an unverifiable acquaintance of an acquaintance | An account verified by a court of law | A regulation issued by municipal authorities | A collection of facts concerning large cities
exam: It is given that a teacher has not arrived fifteen minutes after the commencement of a lesson. Determine the appropriate course of action for the class. | Remain and inform the school office | Depart, pursuant to a rule of fifteen minutes | Return home without delay | Proceed to the playground until the bell
